1: # The actual rotating and Cox pair for the additive pages
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.30 after complete root and independent fourfold audits. The scope is every actual
4: one-cap page in P3K.26 and every actual one- or two-cap page in P3K.28,
5: in the specified vertical-edge whole-\(I_1\) cusp product. It does not
6: assign the relative cycle at a different fan edge.
7: 
8: Write \(M\) for the actual page-cover degree, \(h(G)=1/d\), and let
9: the marked line point be \(aG\). Put
10: \[
11:  H=\frac{Ma^2}{d},\qquad
12:  \chi=\chi(\mathcal O_{S_{\rm page}}).
13: \]
14: In the actual Cox product transported by the extending cusp origin,
15: the rotating pair and the K23 relative coordinates are
16: \[
17:  \begin{split}
18:  k&=-3H,&\ell&=-24\chi,\\
19:  m_{\rm rel}&=6\chi,&n_{\rm rel}&=3H-6\chi,\\
20:  s&=\chi-H/2\pmod{28}.
21:  \end{split}
22:  \tag{K30.1}
23: \]
24: The parameter is the one positive \(r\)-loop with \(\lambda=r^M\).
25: For K26, \(M=m\) and \(\varepsilon=n=\pm1\); for a two-cap K28
26: family, \(M=m_\infty m_0\) and
27: \(\varepsilon=m_0n_\infty+m_\infty n_0=\pm1\).
28: The proof computes the normal clutch before using the index;
29: it does not recover \(k\) from \(A\) alone.
30: 
31: ## 1. The two actual cusp products for the signed parameter
32: 
33: Use the physical coordinates of K26 and K28:
34: \[
35:  V=e^{2\pi i\zeta_2},\qquad
36:  B_\lambda(Z,V)=(U(q)^{-1}Z,q\lambda W(q)^{-1}V),
37: \]
38: where \(U=e^{2\pi i g}\), \(W=e^{-2\pi i C}\) are the actual
39: nonvanishing cusp units. Thus the positive outward period is
40: \[
41:  \gamma:(Z,V)\longmapsto
42:       (U(q)Z,q^{-1}\lambda^{-1}W(q)V).
43:  \tag{K30.2}
44: \]
45: At fixed \(|r|\), variation of the phase of \(\lambda=r^M\)
46: is a physical delta rotation. It disappears in the circle orbit,
47: including in the two actual unimodular Cox charts. Consequently
48: the orbit body and its marked period-cell quotient are fixed
49: before applying the ordinary closing map.
50: 
51: Set \(s=\log q/(2\pi i)\) and \(v=\log r/(2\pi i)\).
52: The actual extending cusp origin is
53: \[
54:  \sigma_\varepsilon(v)
55:        =\varepsilon\left(gv,-Cv-\frac{M(v^2-v)}2\right).
56:  \tag{K30.3}
57: \]
58: The ordinary parameter deck is \(+\varepsilon s\delta\).
59: The identity
60: \[
61:  \sigma_\varepsilon(v+1)-\sigma_\varepsilon(v)
62:             =\varepsilon s\delta+\varepsilon\gamma
63:  \tag{K30.4}
64: \]
65: shows that the ordinary deck minus the cusp-origin difference
66: is the actual integral period \(-\varepsilon\gamma\).
67: This closes the cusp product, with the same positive \(r\)-loop
68: as in the construction proofs.
69: 
70: For a fixed initial parameter \(\lambda_0\), the bounded
71: coordinate map on its universal angular interval is literally
72: \[
73:  C_\theta=
74:  \left(
75:  U^{\varepsilon\theta},
76:  (W\lambda_0^{-1})^{\varepsilon\theta}
77:        e^{-\pi i\varepsilon M\theta(\theta-1)}
78:  \right).
79:  \tag{K30.5}
80: \]
81: All powers use the specified logarithms. These nonzero bounded
82: torus maps extend in the actual two adjacent cusp charts and
83: their Cox master coordinates. At \(\theta=1\) they are exactly
84: the bounded period-return map from (K30.4). The quadratic
85: factor is physical delta; it acts trivially on the orbit normal
86: three-plane. An additional unit in the global ordinary closing
87: map adds the same bounded origin term to both products and
88: is carried by the same extending torus map.
89: 
90: The actual ordinary origin is
91: \[
92:  o_\varepsilon(s,v)
93:        =\varepsilon\left(
94:           -\frac{gs}{M},
95:           \frac{Cs}{M}+\frac{s^2}{2M}+sv\right).
96:  \tag{K30.6}
97: \]
98: The source and parameter descents of these origins, including
99: the auxiliary \(2M\)-root gauge, were proved in K26 and K28.
100: Their literal transition has angular difference
101: \[
102:  (\sigma_\varepsilon-o_\varepsilon)(v+\theta)
103:        -(\sigma_\varepsilon-o_\varepsilon)(v)
104:    =\varepsilon\theta\gamma
105:        -\frac{\varepsilon M\theta(\theta-1)}2\,\delta .
106:  \tag{K30.7}
107: \]
108: On the circle orbit this is the marked rotation
109: \(R_{\varepsilon\theta}\) of the gamma product. Equivalently,
110: it is the positive rotation \(R^\beta_\theta\) for
111: \(\beta=\varepsilon\gamma\).
112: 
113: ## 2. The exact smooth extension and Cox normal comparison
114: 
115: We retain the actual map, rather than differentiate the annular
116: expression involving \(|q|^{\varepsilon\theta}\) at the node.
117: In the first small-resolution chart
118: \[
119:  (x,v,z),\qquad u=xz,\quad y=vz,
120: \]
121: the circle weights on \((v,z)\) are \((-1,+1)\), and its
122: Hopf coordinates are
123: \[
124:                 (y,b),\qquad b=(|z|^2-|v|^2)/2.
125: \]
126: In the second chart \((u,y,w)\), with \(x=uw,v=yw\), use
127: \[
128:                 (x,b'),\qquad b'=(|u|^2-|w|^2)/2.
129: \]
130: On the overlap, putting \(r=|z|^2\), the exact derivatives are
131: \[
132:  2b=r-|y|^2/r,\qquad
133:  2b'=|x|^2r-r^{-1},\qquad
134:  \frac{db'}{db}
135:    =\frac{|x|^2+r^{-2}}{1+|y|^2r^{-2}}>0.
136:  \tag{K30.8}
137: \]
138: The proper increasing orbit coordinate \(\chi_0(x,y,b)\)
139: of P3F.9, fixed relative to its marked outside collar,
140: has \((\chi_0)_b>0\). It gives the smooth product map
141: \(\Phi(x,y,b)=(x,y,\chi_0(x,y,b))\), with the corresponding
142: second-chart formula.
143: 
144: In the two actual extending products the orbit comparison is
145: \[
146:                        J_\theta=R_{\varepsilon\theta}\circ\Phi.
147:  \tag{K30.9}
148: \]
149: Equation (K30.7) proves agreement on the original annular
150: identification; (K30.8) proves the smooth extension through the
151: two node charts. The gamma rotation is an actual flow on the
152: fixed source-preserving product, so its negative direction for
153: \(\varepsilon=-1\) is equally defined.
154: 
155: Quotienting the derivative by the tangent of the fixed curve,
156: including the derivative of its graph gamma coordinate, gives
157: \[
158:  Q_x=
159:  \begin{pmatrix}
160:  1&0&0\\0&1&0\\
161:  (\chi_0)_{\Re y}&(\chi_0)_{\Im y}&(\chi_0)_b
162:  \end{pmatrix},
163:  \qquad(\chi_0)_b>0 .
164:  \tag{K30.10}
165: \]
166: The second chart has the same formula with \(x,b'\).
167: These maps are independent of \(\theta\). Their source shears
168: contract linearly, and the positive last entry contracts to one.
169: The intrinsic normal line is the free gamma direction; the
170: quotient is the source normal two-plane. Its splittings form
171: a contractible space, so the displayed contractions agree
172: globally. This remains true for either signed direction of
173: the transported rotation in (K30.9).
174: 
175: The sign is also retained explicitly in beta coordinates.
176: There the product map is
177: \(\Phi_\beta(x,y,b)=(x,y,\varepsilon\chi_0(x,y,b))\);
178: its raw normal derivative is
179: \[
180:  Q_x^\beta=T_\varepsilon Q_x,\qquad
181:  T_\varepsilon=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,\varepsilon).
182:  \tag{K30.10a}
183: \]
184: For the negative sign its last raw derivative is indeed negative.
185: Apply this same constant coordinate and orientation change to
186: both initial normal frames, as prescribed by the positive-Hopf
187: Thom orientation in K26 and K28. In the transported reference,
188: \(T_\varepsilon^{-1}Q_x^\beta=Q_x\), so the remaining comparison
189: is the positive triangular, theta-independent map just displayed.
190: Equivalently the full relative clutch is conjugated by
191: \(T_\varepsilon\); this is also conjugation by
192: \(\varepsilon T_\varepsilon\in SO(3)\).
193: This gives the actual placement of the signed coordinate change
194: at both ends. It contributes no free three-dimensional
195: framing class.
196: 
197: The initial actual Cox frame is the quaternion frame of K27,
198: which we retain explicitly. If the fixed-sphere charts satisfy
199: \(\zeta'=\zeta^{-1}\), \(c_i'=\zeta c_i\), then
200: \[
201:  q_{\rm H}
202:   =(1+j\zeta)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})
203:   =(\zeta^{-1}+j)(c_0'+j\overline{c_1'}).
204:  \tag{K30.11}
205: \]
206: The two products are literally equal, using \(zj=j\bar z\).
207: The physical delta action is right multiplication by
208: \(e^{-i\theta}\), so the positive Hopf normal coordinate is
209: \(q_{\rm H}i\overline{q_{\rm H}}\), with exactly the K23
210: orientation. Transport this initial frame by \(J_\theta\).
211: By (K30.9), it is the same frame obtained by applying
212: \(dR_{\varepsilon\theta}d\Phi\) to the initial Cox frame.
213: Equation (K30.10) supplies no parameter-dependent free
214: normal-framing class.
215: 
216: The bounded units in (K30.5) also retain their actual normal
217: map. In a Cox chart \(q=\zeta c_0c_1\), a unitary torus map
218: has form
219: \[
220:  (\zeta,c_0,c_1)\longmapsto
221:        (a\zeta,p c_0,q_1c_1),\qquad apq_1=1.
222: \]
223: Choose continuous square roots along the actual interval,
224: \[
225:              A^2=pq_1,\qquad B^2=p/q_1,\qquad AB=p.
226: \]
227: Substitution in (K30.11) gives the literal equality
228: \[
229:                          q_{\rm H}\longmapsto A q_{\rm H}B .
230:  \tag{K30.12}
231: \]
232: Its Hopf normal map is the constant rotation
233: \(\eta\mapsto A\eta\bar A\), independent of the fixed-sphere
234: point. The positive factors are the same normal isomorphism
235: on both sides of the comparison and cancel. The higher
236: base-unit terms have zero normal linear part, because \(q\)
237: is quadratic in \(c_0,c_1\). Thus any remaining loop is a
238: constant rotation loop. It extends over the fixed three-disc
239: and contributes neither \(k\) nor \(\ell\). There is no
240: free \(\pi_3(SO(3))\) discrepancy between the actual closed
241: Cox product and the transported rotating product.
242: 
243: ## 3. The degree-\(M\) binding coordinate is an actual holomorphic chart
244: 
245: For one cap the map in K26 has weighted polynomial
246: \(z_2-\tau z_1^M\), and its source-base coordinate is
247: \(t=z_2/z_1^M\). Its page ray in the binding coordinate
248: \(q_b=t-\tau\) has exactly
249: \[
250:                             \alpha=\phi-M\beta,
251:  \tag{K30.13}
252: \]
253: where \(\phi\) is the fixed outward page phase in radians.
254: 
255: For the two-cap page of K28, its exact ordinary phases give
256: \[
257:  \arg(z_2^{m_0}-\tau z_1^{m_\infty})
258:        =M\beta+\arg(t-\tau)-\varepsilon m_\infty n_0\arg t .
259:  \tag{K30.14}
260: \]
261: Near the regular binding \(t=\tau\ne0\), define the literal
262: holomorphic coordinate
263: \[
264:               q_b=(t-\tau)(t/\tau)^{-\varepsilon m_\infty n_0}.
265:  \tag{K30.15}
266: \]
267: Its derivative at \(\tau\) is one, so it is a local
268: biholomorphism. The exponent is an integer and the factor is
269: a specified nonvanishing holomorphic unit. Equations
270: (K30.14)--(K30.15), after retaining their constant \(\tau\)
271: phase in \(\phi\), prove exactly (K30.13).
272: This retains the actual two-cap source coordinate; it does
273: not replace a variable base phase by its limiting value.
274: 
275: The actual graph of K26 or K28 has
276: \[
277:                          \beta=\theta+g(p)
278:  \tag{K30.16}
279: \]
280: on the binding, with \(p\in T^2\) the source point and
281: \(\theta\) the positive physical parameter angle.
282: Here \(g:T^2\to\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z\) is the graph phase
283: fixed by the source-preserving orbit product. Local radian
284: lifts suffice; \(R_z(-Mg(p))\) is a global map because \(M\)
285: is an integer. No source-dependent modification of the
286: page phase is made.
287: 
288: ## 4. The full normal clutch and its Pontryagin number
289: 
290: Give the graph its oriented page-normal vector and its two
291: Cartesian parameter vectors. The actual graph derivative
292: contributes only a contractible triangular shear to this
293: frame. On the compression choose a normal frame at parameter
294: zero and transport it by \(R_{\varepsilon\theta}\).
295: The compression is the same solid torus minus a ball used
296: in K6. Its tangent bundle is trivial, and
297: \(TY\oplus\mathbb R\) is trivial for the actual orbit
298: \(Y=S^5\). Hence its rank-three oriented normal bundle has
299: \(w_2=0\), and the three-dimensional obstruction groups
300: give a frame. At the fixed-sphere end its difference from
301: the specified positive Hopf frame is a map
302: \(S^2\to SO(3)\), which extends across the fixed three-disc.
303: Transport this actual extension by the same signed flow.
304: 
305: After quotienting by the source tangent directions and
306: the graph tangent, use the four coordinates consisting of
307: the real and imaginary \(q_b\) directions, the parameter
308: radius, and the relative angle \(\beta-\theta\).
309: The outward page collar and inward compression tangent
310: point in the same positive radial half-space. Their actual
311: common tangent is
312: \[
313:            u=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha,\rho,0),\qquad\rho>0.
314: \]
315: The literal quotient map is
316: \[
317:  (v_q,v_r,v_\eta)\longmapsto
318:        \left(v_q-\frac{v_r}{\rho}(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha),
319:                v_\eta\right).
320:  \tag{K30.17}
321: \]
322: It kills \(u\). Project the Cartesian parameter vectors,
323: whose radial-relative coefficients are
324: \((\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)\), \((\sin\theta,\cos\theta)\);
325: reverse both and apply the positive reference rescalings.
326: The resulting actual oriented graph normal matrix is
327: \[
328:  G(\alpha,\theta)=
329:  \begin{pmatrix}
330:  -\sin\alpha&\cos\theta\cos\alpha&\sin\theta\cos\alpha\\
331:  \cos\alpha&\cos\theta\sin\alpha&\sin\theta\sin\alpha\\
332:  0&\sin\theta&-\cos\theta
333:  \end{pmatrix}\in SO(3).
334:  \tag{K30.18}
335: \]
336: The signed Thom calculation of K26 and K28 fixes the normal
337: orientation here as positive Hopf for both signs.
338: Equivalently, changing from the gamma relative coordinate
339: to the beta relative coordinate multiplies the reference
340: three-space by the constant matrix
341: \(T_\varepsilon=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,\varepsilon)\).
342: It conjugates both frame comparisons. This conjugation is
343: also conjugation by the oriented constant matrix
344: \(\varepsilon T_\varepsilon\in SO(3)\), so it introduces no
345: additional clutch or Pontryagin term.
346: 
347: The derivatives of \(g(p)\) are exactly the source tangent
348: shears already removed in (K30.17). The positive \(\rho\)
349: rescaling is contractible, and the actual collar rounding
350: stays in the same contractible positive radial half-space.
351: These specify a homotopy of the actual transition, including
352: all source tangent terms.
353: 
354: In the transported product coordinates the compression
355: frame is \(h(p)\), independent of \(\theta\). Since
356: \(G(\alpha,\theta)=R_z(\alpha)G(0,\theta)\), the full
357: clutch on the actual \(T^3\) is homotopic to
358: \[
359:  \begin{split}
360:  &h(p)^{-1}G(\phi-M(\theta+g(p)),\theta)\\
